 "is_vocab": false,
 "stimulus_html": "<p>“The arbitrary separation of citizens, on the basis of race, while they are on a public highway, is a badge of servitude wholly inconsistent with the civil freedom and the equality before the law established by the Constitution. It cannot be justified upon any legal grounds.</p>\n<p>If evils will result from the commingling of the two races upon public highways established for the benefit of all, they will be infinitely less than those that will surely come from state legislation regulating the enjoyment of civil rights upon the basis of race. We boast of the freedom enjoyed by our people all other peoples. But it is difficult to reconcile that boast with the state of the law which, practically, puts the brand of servitude and degradation upon a large class of our fellow citizens, our equals before the law. The thin disguise of ‘equal’ accommodations for passengers in railroad coaches will not mislead anyone, nor atone for the wrong this day done.”</p>\n<p align=\"right\">Justice John Marshall Harlan, dissenting opinion in <i>Plessy v. Ferguson</i> 163 U.S. 537, (1896)</p>\n<p><i>Refer to the excerpt provided.</i></p>",
 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 615706,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "The overarching concept of Justice Harlan’s dissenting opinion in <i>Plessy v. Ferguson</i> can best be stated as",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 1170793,
     "content_html": "the Constitution is color-blind"
    },
    {
     "id": 1170792,
     "content_html": "states’ rights are superior to the power of the federal government"
    },
    {
     "id": 1170791,
     "content_html": "the Constitution provides for women’s suffrage"
    },
    {
     "id": 1170790,
     "content_html": "equal access to education is paramount"
    }
   ],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "multiple-choice"
   ],
   "combo_choices": []
  },
  {
   "id": 615707,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "During the time as the <i>Plessy v. Ferguson</i> decision, some members of the African American community were engaged in",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 1170796,
     "content_html": "opening vocational schools like Tuskegee Institute"
    },
    {
     "id": 1170797,
     "content_html": "documenting lynching incidents across the country"
    },
    {
     "id": 1170795,
     "content_html": "organizing civil rights marches"
    },
    {
     "id": 1170794,
     "content_html": "boycotting places of business that practiced discrimination based on race"
    }
   ],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "multiple-choice"
   ],
   "combo_choices": []
  },
  {
   "id": 615708,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "Which of these statements is an accurate paraphrase of the excerpt?",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 1170800,
     "content_html": "The separation of citizens on the basis of race in public places is consistent with the Constitution as long as the separate facilities are equal."
    },
    {
     "id": 1170799,
     "content_html": "Failure to properly regulate the enjoyment of civil rights in a state would be evil."
    },
    {
     "id": 1170801,
     "content_html": "Boasting of freedom does not make sense as long as the law itself degrades our fellow citizens."
    },
    {
     "id": 1170798,
     "content_html": "Providing for equal accommodations in the future will be sufficient to correct any inequality today."
    }
   ],
